WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.017 --> 00:00:02.100
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Welcome
to Google Cloud Drawing

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:04.590
Board, where we learn our
way through the cloud.

00:00:04.590 --> 00:00:07.830
Today's topic-- what is
Cloud Load Balancing?

00:00:07.830 --> 00:00:09.750
This video is divided
into chapters.

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:11.460
Watch the full
video or skip ahead

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:13.600
to any section of your choice.

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:16.410
Sam's new application
has been a hit.

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:20.400
Users are growing across the
world, but Sam is worried.

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:23.340
How would she scale,
optimize, and secure the app,

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:25.350
while keeping the users happy?

00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:28.200
That's where Cloud Load
Balancing comes in.

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:31.680
It is a fully-distributed,
software-defined solution

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:35.220
that balances user traffic
to multiple backends

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:38.250
to avoid congestion
and ensure low latency.

00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:40.110
There are different
types of load balancing,

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:42.480
depending on the type of
traffic you are dealing with,

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:44.380
global or regional.

00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:47.430
Let's understand these
options with a use case.

00:00:47.430 --> 00:00:50.430
Say you have user
Shen in California.

00:00:50.430 --> 00:00:53.480
You deploy your backend
instances in that region

00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:55.870
and configure a
load-balancing virtual IP.

00:00:55.870 --> 00:00:58.710
When your new users
grow to another region,

00:00:58.710 --> 00:01:01.260
all you need to do is
to create instances

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:02.750
in the additional regions.

00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:06.630
There is no change in the
virtual IP or the DNS service

00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:07.500
settings.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:11.400
As your app grows global,
the same pattern can follow.

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:13.770
You see that Maya
from India is routed

00:01:13.770 --> 00:01:15.660
to the instances in India.

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:18.600
Now, if the instances
in India are overloaded

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:21.240
and are autoscaling
to handle the load,

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:24.450
that user will
seamlessly be redirected

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:27.060
to the other instance
in the meantime,

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:29.280
and routed back to
India when instances

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:32.190
have scaled sufficiently
to handle that load.

00:01:32.190 --> 00:01:34.380
We just saw that
Cloud Load Balancing

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:39.250
uses anycast virtual IPs, giving
you a single global frontend

00:01:39.250 --> 00:01:40.780
virtual IP address.

00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:44.880
It also provides cross-regional
failover, fast autoscaling,

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:47.790
and scales to millions
of queries per second.

00:01:47.790 --> 00:01:50.880
That is external load
balancing at layer 7.

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:53.700
But in any three-tier
app, after the frontend,

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:55.890
you have the middleware
and the data sources

00:01:55.890 --> 00:01:59.040
to interact with in order
to fulfill a user's request.

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:02.100
That's where you need
layer 4, internal load

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:06.030
balancing between the frontend
and the other internal tiers.

00:02:06.030 --> 00:02:10.919
Layer 4 internal load balancing
is really for TCP/UDP traffic

00:02:10.919 --> 00:02:16.110
behind RFC 1918 VIP, where
the client IP is preserved.

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:18.150
You'll get the
automatic health checks,

00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:20.190
and there's really
no middle proxy.

00:02:20.190 --> 00:02:22.590
It leverages the
software-defined networking

00:02:22.590 --> 00:02:26.320
controls and data plane
for load balancing.

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:29.580
Now let's see how the data
model for Cloud Load Balancing

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:31.020
actually works.

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:33.720
For global HTTPS
load balancing, you

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:37.800
have the global anycast
virtual IPs, IPv4 or IPv6,

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:41.430
associated with the forwarding
rule, which directs traffic

00:02:41.430 --> 00:02:42.940
to a target proxy.

00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:45.870
The target proxy terminates
the client's session.

00:02:45.870 --> 00:02:49.110
And for HTTPS, you deploy
your certificates here.

00:02:49.110 --> 00:02:52.410
This is not a single device,
but distributed logic

00:02:52.410 --> 00:02:54.060
throughout the infrastructure.

00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:58.020
The URL map configured
provides layer 7 routing

00:02:58.020 --> 00:03:00.990
and directs the client request
to the appropriate backend

00:03:00.990 --> 00:03:01.860
service.

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:04.650
The backend services can
be managed instance groups

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:08.020
or network endpoint groups for
your containerized workloads.

00:03:08.020 --> 00:03:11.010
This is also where service
capacity and health

00:03:11.010 --> 00:03:14.670
is determined, and Cloud CDN
is enabled to cache content

00:03:14.670 --> 00:03:16.500
for improved performance.

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:19.050
You can set up firewall
rules to control traffic

00:03:19.050 --> 00:03:21.120
to and from your backend here.

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:24.330
Internal load balancing
setup works the same way.

00:03:24.330 --> 00:03:28.260
We still have a forwarding rule,
but here, it points directly

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:30.090
to a backend service.

00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:33.750
The forwarding rule has the
virtual IP address, protocol,

00:03:33.750 --> 00:03:35.320
and up to five ports.

00:03:35.320 --> 00:03:38.280
Now, security-- as
a best practice,

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:41.850
we encourage you to
run SSL everywhere.

00:03:41.850 --> 00:03:45.030
With HTTPS and SSL
proxy load balancing,

00:03:45.030 --> 00:03:47.280
you can use
Google-managed certs,

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:49.260
where Google takes care
of the provisioning

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:52.830
and managing the SSL
certificate lifecycle for you.

00:03:52.830 --> 00:03:55.620
And Cloud Load Balancing
supports multiple SSL

00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:57.360
certificates, as
well, if you wanted

00:03:57.360 --> 00:04:01.080
to serve multiple domains using
the same load-balancing IP

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:02.520
address and port.

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:05.400
Global load balancing
absorbs and dissipates

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:08.760
layer 3, 4 volumetric
attacks across Google's

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:11.220
global load-balancing
infrastructure.

00:04:11.220 --> 00:04:13.620
Additionally, by
using Cloud Armor,

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:17.970
you can protect against layer 3
to 7 application level attacks.

00:04:17.970 --> 00:04:21.329
And by using Identity
Aware Proxy and firewalls,

00:04:21.329 --> 00:04:23.640
you can authenticate
and authorize access

00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:25.800
to your backends.

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:28.410
How to choose the right
load-balancing option?

00:04:28.410 --> 00:04:31.080
For deciding which
load-balancing option is right

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:35.250
for you, consider factors such
as internal versus external,

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:38.400
global versus regional,
type of traffic--

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:40.980
HTTPS, TLS, UDP.

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:42.990
And based on these
factors, decide

00:04:42.990 --> 00:04:44.560
which one is right for you.

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:47.700
If you are looking to reduce
latency, improve performance,

00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:51.090
security, and optimize cost
for your backend systems,

00:04:51.090 --> 00:04:53.040
then check out Cloud
Load Balancing.

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:56.670
It is easy to deploy in just a
few clicks, set up the frontend

00:04:56.670 --> 00:04:59.070
and backends associated
with the global VIPs,

00:04:59.070 --> 00:05:00.780
and you're good to go.

00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:02.020
Want to learn more?

00:05:02.020 --> 00:05:05.160
Check out
cloud.google.com/loadbalancing.

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:08.210
